        <li>
          <title>Course Description</title>

          <p>
            Differential equations as a subfield of mathematics furthers the concept of modeling phenomena using functions. In MATH 340 Intro to Differential Equations, we revisit the derivative <m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m> of a differentiable function <m>x</m> with respect to a single independent variable such as <m>t</m> for time. 
            We assume that <m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m>, <m>\frac{d^{2}x}{{dt}^{2}}</m>, and so on are also differentiable functions. &#xA;  

            Equations (e.g. <m>\frac{dx}{dt} = x</m>) with an unknown function <m>x</m> written in terms of <m>x</m>, <m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m>, <m>\frac{d^{2}x}{{dt}^{2}}</m>, etc., are called ordinary differential equations (ODEs). We consider numerical, analytic, and qualitative methods for finding and describing solution functions (e.g. <m>x(t) = e^{t}</m>) to ODEs. We focus especially on first-order and second-order ODEs. To begin, we discuss types of ODEs and their importance in modeling the world around us.
          </p>

        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Knowledge Prerequisite</title>

          <p>
            Students should be familiar with linearization and the derivative, review series and approximation (Taylor series), and demonstrate algebraic savvy.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Textbook and course materials</title>

          <p>
            We use the freely available <url href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1SW_IQxnIWSiHKEL9XT530DUdmww2zvM4/view?usp=drive_link">
            <pubtitle>A First Course in Differential Equations Third Edition</pubtitle></url>
            by John D. Logan. Use Morgan.edu email to access the link to the book.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

